      <div type="clause" n="15">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Hn">Hn</ref> 15</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hn 15" corresp="#wit-lat"><ptr type="liebermannNote" n="24" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.24"/><pb n="559" ed="#liebermann"/>De Denegildo<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="25" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.25"/>. Denagildum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="26" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.26"/>, quod aliquando þingemannis<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="27" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.27"/> dabatur, id est XII den. de unaquaque hyda<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="28" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.28"/> per annum, si ad terminos<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="29" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.29"/> non reddatur, wita<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="30" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.30"/> emendetur<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="31" target="#Hn_15.lnote.oe.31"/>.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 559 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.24" n="24" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">C. XVI <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">C. XVI Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.25" n="25" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Dan. <hi rend="italic">K Co Sl Tw;</hi> Danag. <hi rend="italic">Or</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Dan. K Co Sl Tw; Danag. Or</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.26" n="26" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Dan. <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Dan. Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.27" n="27" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ying. <hi rend="italic">Gi;</hi> yingeman <hi rend="italic">Co Or; Sp s. v.</hi> danigeld; þingeman <hi rend="italic">K;</hi> ringe manus <hi rend="italic">Wh Wi;</hi> Runicis <hi rend="italic">oder</hi> wicing-mannis <hi rend="italic">empfiehlt irrig Somner</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ying. Gi; yingeman Co Or; Sp s. v. danigeld; þingeman K; ringe manus Wh Wi; Somner wrongly recommends Runicis or wicing-mannis</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.28" n="28" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">hida <hi rend="italic">Or Wi Sp</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">hida Or Wi Sp</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.29" n="29" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-num <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-num Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.30" n="30" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">wyta <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">wyta Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.31" n="31" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">559</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">enim detur <hi rend="italic">K;</hi> emind. <hi rend="italic">Co</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">enim detur K; emind. Co</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.a" n="a" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">319</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Wort (gegen Steenstrup und Larson <hi rend="italic">King's household</hi> 154), zu scheiden von <hi rend="italic">þegnung(þenig)-men</hi> (Toller; Sweet; Kemble <hi rend="italic">Cod. dipl</hi>. 314. 1258), das freilich auch ‘kriegerischer Dienstmann’ bedeutet, kommt von <hi rend="italic">þings</hi> ‘cuneus’, German. Heeresorgan. Die Engländer entlehnten es, wie für die stehende Flotte <hi rend="italic">liðsmen, butscarlas</hi>, den Nordleuten.—<hi rend="italic">De Danegildo</hi>{25}. <hi rend="italic">Danag., q a. þingeman</hi>. Rs</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The word (pace Steenstrup and Larson <hi rend="italic">King's household</hi> 154), which is to be distinguished from <hi rend="italic">þegnung(þenig)-men</hi> (Toller; Sweet; Kemble <hi rend="italic">Cod. dipl</hi>. 314. 1258) — though that admittedly also means ‘warlike retainer’ — comes from <hi rend="italic">þings</hi> ‘cuneus’, a Germanic army body. The English borrowed it from the Northmen, as they did <hi rend="italic">liðsmen, butscarlas</hi> for the standing fleet.—<hi rend="italic">De Danegildo</hi>{25}. <hi rend="italic">Danag., q a. þingeman</hi>. Rs</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.b" n="b" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">319</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Wie ECf verschweigt Verf. den Ursprung des Dänengeldes (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 2 ff. 7) aus dem Tribut an die Feinde und kennt nur den Einen Hufen-Schilling jährlich</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Like ECf, the author passes over in silence the origin of the Danegeld (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 2 ff. 7) in the tribute paid to the enemy, and knows only the single shilling per hide yearly</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_15.lnote.oe.c" n="c" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">319</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Wort <hi rend="italic">gyltwite</hi> in Urk. a. 964 Birch *1135 hat nichts mit <hi rend="italic">geld</hi> oder einer Steuer zu tun, sondern bed. ‘Schuld(Verbrechen)strafe’</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The word <hi rend="italic">gyltwite</hi> in a charter a. 964, Birch *1135, has nothing to do with <hi rend="italic">geld</hi> or a tax, but means ‘penalty for guilt (crime)’</seg></note>
      </div>
